WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.610
My name is Eric Bersin,
and I am a graduate student

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.730
in the quantum
photonics group at MIT.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:07.710
And my research in particular
deals with quantum computing.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.930
So quantum computing is
a new type of computing

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.150
that harnesses the principles
of quantum mechanics

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:15.340
to do computations in a
fundamentally different way.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.970
And in particular, this allows
for more powerful calculations

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.490
of certain types of
problems, ranging from things

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.090
like factoring large prime
numbers to calculating

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.160
interactions for biochemistry.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.600
And so quantum
computing has been

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.350
around for some
number of years now.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:30.933
And we're starting
to get to the point

00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:33.450
where we actually have systems
of multiple quantum bits

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.549
or qubits.

00:00:34.549 --> 00:00:36.090
And we're starting
to try to put them

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.120
into large numbers of systems
on chip in particular.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.646
And quantum
computing is actually

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:42.270
experiencing a lot
of the same problems

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.811
that classical
computing experienced,

00:00:43.811 --> 00:00:46.287
namely that when you have lots
of systems all in one chip,

00:00:46.287 --> 00:00:48.870
very close together, you start
to have problems with crosstalk

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.300
and problems and trying to
address individual qubits

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.417
at the same time.

00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:54.000
And so my work in
particular is trying

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.690
to look at how we can develop
new techniques for individually

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.989
addressing systems of qubits.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:00.780
And particularly the
system that we look at

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.240
is a nitrogen vacancy
center in diamond.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.670
So essentially this is a
defect, a single atom defect

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.660
inside of a crystal lattice.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.750
And so that helps
us get pretty far.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.430
So we have single
atom qubits, which

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.574
is pretty small
and pretty compact.

00:01:13.574 --> 00:01:15.740
But it turns out that
actually to talk these qubits,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.279
you need to use lasers
and optical addressing.

00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:20.070
And for those who are
familiar with optics,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.040
you may know that
there's something called

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.260
the diffraction
limit, which means

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.240
that the smallest you
can make a laser spot

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.290
is roughly half the wavelength.

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.260
And so that sort of places
a fundamental limit,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.470
you would think, on how narrowly
you can address these qubits,

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.420
and how tightly
you can pack them.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.040
So what we were able
to help actually

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.200
through part of my master's
work was the technique

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.290
for using small differences
between these qubits

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.540
and slight differences in
their transition frequencies

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.940
as a result of their environment
to individually address

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.250
qubits with very, very high
precision and high fidelity.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.770
And so we showed
that we could have

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.870
an ensemble of multiple
different qubits,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.480
these little atom like
defects in diamond

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.040
and address a
single one at a time

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.810
without perturbing the
state of the other.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.330
So we think this is really a
path forward towards having

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:04.770
really scalable systems with
very densely packed information

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.627
on a single chip for
quantum computing.

